The original does a linear scan over all `MESGS` entries on every call. This version builds a 64K-entry index once and then answers each lookup with a single array access.

- **When it is built:** a function-local `static`, initialized once on first use. C++11 guarantees this initialization is thread-safe.
- **What it depends on:** only the constant `mesgs` table, not any mutable global state.
- **Same results:** each slot stores the first matching index, so duplicate message numbers resolve to the same entry as the linear scan. Numbers with no match return `NULL`, as before.
- **Fallback:** if the table were ever too large for the 16-bit index, every slot stays at the sentinel and the original linear scan runs instead. This keeps behavior identical in that case.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    static const std::array<FIT_UINT16, 65536> table = []()
    {
        std::array<FIT_UINT16, 65536> t;
        t.fill(0xFFFF);
        if (MESGS < 0xFFFF)
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                if (t[n] == 0xFFFF)
                    t[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
            }
        }
        return t;
    }();

    if (MESGS < 0xFFFF)
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx = table[num];
        if (idx == 0xFFFF)
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[idx];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```

```includes
#include <array>
```